## Why This Is Asked
Test ability to count bits with a constant-time sequence of masks and shifts, no loops.

## Key Concepts
- Parallel bit counting
- Fixed masks: 0x5555..., 0x3333..., 0x0F0F...
- No branches

## Code Example
```javascript
function popcount64(x) {
  // x is a BigInt
  x = x - ((x >> 1n) & 0x5555555555555555n);
  x = (x & 0x3333333333333333n) + ((x >> 2n) & 0x3333333333333333n);
  x = (x + (x >> 4n)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0Fn;
  x = x + (x >> 8n);
  x = x + (x >> 16n);
  x = x + (x >> 32n);
  return Number(x & 0x7Fn);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this for 128-bit using the same pattern?
- What are the performance implications on 32-bit vs 64-bit targets?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests understanding of multi-limb bit tricks and how two's complement generalizes to 128 bits without branches.

## Key Concepts

- x & (-x) principle for lsb isolation across 2 limbs
- careful carry propagation from low to high limb
- avoiding branches by using arithmetic comparisons that yield 0/1

## Code Example

```c
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;

uint128 isolateLowestSetBit128(uint128 x) {
  uint64_t loNeg = ~x.lo + 1;
  uint64_t carry = (loNeg < ~x.lo);
  uint64_t hiNeg = ~x.hi + carry;
  uint128 r;
  r.lo = x.lo & loNeg;
  r.hi = x.hi & hiNeg;
  return r;
}
```

